for . Note that, when , we have .\n\nThe generalised pancake graph (see [4]) is the undirected Cayley graph , where In other words, the vertices of the graph are all m–coloured permutations where two vertices are adjacent if and only if one can be obtained from the other by applying a prefix reversal {r}_{k}^{\\epsilon }. In particular, is the classical pancake graph, while is the burnt pancake graph in which “adding ” coincides with changing the sign of each flipped entry. For the graph is 2n–regular (two flips for each ), whereas for it is n–regular (the two flips coincide). In Fig. 1, the graph is illustrated. The vertices are partitioned into vertex subsets for each and ; these subsets are demarcated by trapezia. Within each trapezium, the vertex closest to the central hexagon corresponds to the coloured permutation in which 2 has colour 0. Proceeding clockwise from this vertex around the trapezium yields first the coloured permutation in which 2 has colour 1, followed by the coloured permutation in which 2 has colour 2.\n\nPancake graphs and their generalisations exhibit diverse properties, such as minimal flip counts [5], pancyclicity [3] and applications in genome rearrangement [16], parallel computing [15], and information theory [14]. In genomics, pancake flipping models chromosomal inversions, where extensive DNA segments reverse orientation, a process analogous to the burnt pancake sorting [12], [13].\n\nWe address three spectral properties of the graphs : the spectral gap, eigenvalue multiplicity, and integral eigenvalues. Our motivation comes from recent work [2], [4] of Blanco and Buehrle on the integral spectra of burnt pancake graphs and generalised pancake graphs. In this note, we prove both [2, Conjecture 9(i)] (Theorem 1.1) and [4, Conjecture 6.1] (Theorem 1.2). We denote by the ith largest eigenvalue of the adjacency matrix of Γ.\n\nTheorem 1.1 Blanco-Buehrle's spectral gap conjecture\n\nLet be integers. Then\n\nTheorem 1.1 also improves on [4, Theorem 4.1], which implies an upper bound on the spectral gap: .\n\nFor a graph Γ with eigenvalue λ, denote by the multiplicity of λ as an eigenvalue of the adjacency matrix of Γ.\n\nTheorem 1.2\n\nLet and . Then\n\nThe key to our proofs is a simple observation about the form of the quotient matrix of an equitable partition of (see Lemma 2.5). We note in passing that, even though the graphs are Cayley graphs, since the set of prefix reversals is not conjugation-closed, it is difficult to apply character theory [9], [17] directly to compute their eigenvalues.\n\n\n## Section snippets\n\n### Equitable partitions and interlacing\n\nGiven a real symmetric matrix M, we denote its eigenvalues in nonincreasing order .\n\nLemma 2.1\n\n### [7]\n\nLet A be an real symmetric matrix and B be an principal submatrix of A. Then, for each ,\n\nGiven a graph , a partition of V is called equitable if, for all , the numbers do not depend on the choice of . These numbers, independent of the vertex, are denoted by {q}_{ij} and form the quotient matrix\n\n## Declaration of Competing Interest\n\nI hereby declare that I have no conflicts of interest that could have influenced the work presented in this document. If any potential conflicts of interest arise in the future, I will disclose them promptly.\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nThe authors thank the referees for their careful reading and helpful suggestions, which have improved the paper. GG was supported by the Singapore Ministry of Education Academic Research Fund; grant numbers: RG14/24 (Tier 1) and MOE-T2EP20222-0005 (Tier 2).\n\n## References (17)\n\n  * H. Bass et al.\n\n### Eigenvalues of symmetric matrices and graphs\n\n### J. Algebra\n\n(1994)\n\n  * S.A. Blanco et al.\n\n### Some integer values in the spectra of burnt pancake graphs\n\n### Linear Algebra Appl.\n\n(2024)\n\n  * S.A. Blanco et al.\n\n### Lengths of cycles in generalized pancake graphs\n\n### Discrete Math.\n\n(2023)\n\n  * S.A. Blanco et al.\n\n### On the spectra of